## Context

Goal 2 of the ladder (`README.md`): *the cost of staying current is what
changed, two server processes on one data directory do not corrupt or duplicate,
and an index under another schema version ends up served.* Three terms: **R3,
R13, R23**. Second rung because these need a built index but not a good one.

This tracker holds one question: **is goal 2 kept for zoteus?** Today no clause
of it has been decided at all.

## Where the rung stands for zoteus

From `bench/results/0604-ladder-matrix/MATRIX.md`, 2026-09-03, revision 1.12.0 —
every cell `not-run`, and the reasons differ:

| clause | why not decided |
|---|---|
| `R3-edit-recomputes-only-what-changed` | no `work.<stage>.<trigger>.<outcome>` counters to read |
| `R3-identical-resync-recomputes-nothing` | idem |
| `R13-two-processes-both-answer` | no process reported a hit list |
| `R13-two-processes-do-not-duplicate-work` | no counters |
| `R23-foreign-stamp-ends-up-serving` | the target answers without reporting what it matched |

Four of five turn on one absence, which is why the counters are the largest
single unblock on the whole board and not only this rung's.

## The gap this tracker owns that 0033 does not

**A non-destructive resume, required by R3 and R4 — not by R22.** zoteus's only
action that continues an interrupted build is also the full rebuild, and on a
damaged index it deletes the unreadable file first. Continuing therefore
re-embeds every byte that did not change, which is the sentence R3 exists to
forbid: *"The cost of staying current MUST be proportional to what changed,
never to the size of the library … MUST re-embed nothing whose bytes are
unchanged."* Deleting the index before rebuilding also strains R4's *"the index
MUST answer queries at every moment of its life"*.

Recorded because it was nearly mis-filed: the ratified seven-verb interface
names `resume`, and the adapter declares it absent, which invites reading the
gap as an interface gap on goal 1's rung. **An interface verb is not a promise.**
The promise is R3's, and R3 sits here.

**And it is the hard one of the three, for a reason worth stating.** The other
two gaps found by the 2026-09-03 reread are contained: an uninstall surface
removes a declared set of paths, and R22's restart clause needs a measurement
before it needs any code at all. A resume that neither rebuilds nor deletes is
not contained — it requires the system to know, per item and per stage, what was
finished, what was in flight when the stop arrived, and what is downstream of a
key that has since changed. That is the same substrate the counters need: a
ledger whose unit of invalidation is (item × stage), which is R3's own body
("the unit of invalidation is (item × stage)") and is what ticket 0033 builds.

So gap 3 and the counters are **one piece of work seen from two sides** — resume
asks the ledger *what is left to do*, the counters ask it *what has been done* —
and neither is a small contained contribution of the kind `SYNC.md` records
going upstream as a pull request. That argues for the issue form the maintainer
builds himself, and it argues against scheduling gap 3 as though it were a
sibling of the uninstall surface.

## Driving the pause path — the one open question left on R3

Source read (not just the tool docstring) confirms the pause path is meant to
share the checkpoint mechanism with the crash/timeout path:
`index-manager.ts`'s `buildIncremental` comment says "a timeout, crash, or
`requestStop()` never leaves a corrupt index and whatever was saved stays
queryable"; `requestStop()` (line 742) only flips a cooperative
`cancelToken.cancelled`, and the checkpoint write (`noteCheckpoint()`, line
1370) fires `if (token.cancelled || this.embedderError)` — one branch, no
distinction between why the token was cancelled. That is strong design-level
evidence, but nobody has driven it: no run exists where `action:"stop"` was
issued mid-build and a following `action:"build"` was observed to resume
rather than redo.

### Relevant files
- `bench/run_build.py` — existing one-shot driver (start → poll to terminal →
  terminate). Reusable for phase 1 (start, watch progress) but has no path to
  issue `action:"stop"` mid-poll or to run a second build against a
  process it already terminated; needs a small new script alongside it rather
  than a flag bent to fit, since the two-phase shape (interrupt, then a
  *second* server process against the same data-dir) is a different control
  flow, not a parametrization of the existing one.
- `bench/mcp_drive.py` — the `Server` class (stdio JSON-RPC) is the piece to
  reuse directly: two `Server(...)` instances against the same `--data-dir`,
  the first killed mid-build, the second issuing `action:"build"` again.
- `fork/src/tools/index-tool.ts` — `action:"stop"` handler (line 65) and the
  tool docstring's own claims about `resumedFrom`.
- `fork/src/features/search/index-manager.ts` — `requestStop()` (742),
  `buildIncremental` resume logic (918-975), checkpoint save (1370).

### Actions
1. Wait for the running `bench/results/0605-padme-full-index/` build to reach
   a terminal state (`done`/`error`) before starting anything below — same
   live Zotero desktop, same local API, and a concurrent second crawl would
   contend for it regardless of which build-sizing option is chosen.
2. Write `bench/pause_resume_probe.py` (or similar), built on
   `mcp_drive.Server`, not a hand-rolled JSON-RPC client:
   - Phase 1: launch a server against a **fresh, isolated** data-dir
     (`bench/results/0614-pause-resume/data-dir`, not padme's real one and
     not 0605's), `ZOTEUS_INDEX_MAX_ITEMS` capped small (start at 300 —
     large enough that metadata+fulltext crawl takes tens of seconds at
     `--poll 1`, small enough to iterate fast and stay clear of the running
     build's item scale), embeddings off (keyword+fulltext only — the
     checkpoint mechanism under test is item/page-granular, not
     vector-granular, and gap 3's own upstream fix already exercised the
     vector side per the 12:38Z log entry). Issue `action:"build"`.
   - Poll `action:"status"` every ~1s. Once `itemsFetched` is between ~40%
     and ~80% of the cap (partial commit, crawl demonstrably in flight —
     `fulltextItemsScanned` > 0 is an even stronger midpoint if the metadata
     pass is too fast to catch), issue `action:"stop"`.
   - Poll until `state` reaches a terminal value (cancellation is
     cooperative, per `requestStop()`'s own comment: halts *after* the
     current page/batch). Record: itemsFetched/items committed, passage
     count, `resumedFrom` (must be absent — this is the interrupted run, not
     a resume), wall-clock elapsed for phase 1.
   - **Kill the phase-1 server process** (not just let the driver's own
     `finally` clean it up — kill it explicitly and confirm exit) before
     phase 2, rather than reusing the same process. Reading `action:"build"`
     resume off a *second* process forces the checkpoint to survive a real
     process boundary — read back from the sqlite meta row, not from an
     in-memory field the first process happened to keep — which is the
     stronger and more relevant test given R4's restart language, and it is
     what actually distinguishes this from "the same running instance
     remembered its own state," a much weaker claim than what R3 needs.
   - Phase 2: launch a **new** server against the **same** data-dir, same
     env. Issue `action:"build"` again (not `"refresh"` — refresh is
     documented to always restart). Poll to terminal. Record: `resumedFrom`,
     final itemsFetched/items/passage counts, wall-clock elapsed for phase 2.
3. Compare phase 1 and phase 2 records against the discriminating control
   below and write the verdict into this ticket's log, with the raw before/
   after counts and elapsed times quoted (not just "resumed: yes/no").

### Test / discriminating control
The risk named by this raid's brief: a coincidence where phase 2 "looks like"
a resume (finishes, index ends up complete) while actually having silently
redone work — final counts converge to the same complete library either way,
so final-state-only checks cannot tell resume from restart-from-scratch that
happened to also finish. Two independent signals, not one, because either
alone has a plausible innocent explanation:
- **`resumedFrom` must be present and approximately equal to the item count
  committed at the moment `action:"stop"` was issued in phase 1.** This is
  the direct signal (`index-manager.ts:971`, `known.size` — items already in
  the store when the resumed build starts) — not inferred from timing. Its
  absence, or a value near 0, means phase 2 did not treat phase 1's work as
  already done, regardless of how fast it finished.
- **Phase 2 elapsed time must be substantially less than a full fresh build
  of the same item cap** (a full crawl+fulltext pass over 300 items, timed
  separately if phase 1's own elapsed time before the stop is not already a
  usable proxy for "cost of the un-resumed remainder"). A resume that only
  redoes the last in-flight page/batch should be a small fraction of a full
  build's time; if phase 2 takes roughly as long as phase 1 took to reach
  the same point, that is the tell of a silent full redo.
Only both together clear the bar. `resumedFrom` alone could in principle be
misreported without the underlying skip actually happening (the field is
just a counter); elapsed time alone is noisy and could be fast by luck on a
small cap. Neither risk survives both signals agreeing.

### Verification
- [ ] phase 1 stop is confirmed honored: status reaches a terminal state
      after `action:"stop"`, and process CPU activity stops (goal-1's R22
      tracker already establishes how to observe this — reuse it rather than
      inventing a second method)
- [ ] phase 2 `resumedFrom` recorded and compared to phase-1's committed
      count at stop time
- [ ] phase 2 elapsed time recorded and compared to a full-build reference
- [ ] verdict (resumes / does not resume / inconclusive-and-why) logged on
      this ticket with the raw numbers

### Invariants
- Never run this probe while `bench/results/0605-padme-full-index/` (or any
  other build against the real padme library) is active — same Zotero
  desktop local API, single process, contention risk stated in this raid's
  brief.
- The probe's data-dir is disposable and separate from every other bench
  result directory; it must not touch padme's real Zotero data or the 0605
  results.

## Depends on

- **ticket 0033** — the ledger, freshness and counters. Four of this rung's five
  cells cannot be read until it lands. This tracker does not restate its scope.
- **ticket 0613** — goal 1. Not a blocker in the erg sense: the rungs are ordered
  by build order, not by dependency, and README records that the durability rung
  precedes goal 1 in one direction while goal 1 is numbered first.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] all five goal-2 clauses green for zoteus, each resting on a run
- [ ] the resume gap has a decided upstream form and is filed, or is recorded as
      deliberately not filed with the reason

## Why one tracker per rung, per target

The ladder's rungs are conjunctions: a rung is kept when every one of its members
holds, and at no state before that. Nothing in the repository asserted that
conjunction *for a named target* — `README.md` reports each requirement's
standing across the project, and `bench/results/0604-ladder-matrix/MATRIX.md`
reports each clause against each target, but neither answers "is this rung kept
for zoteus". These three trackers answer exactly that, one each, and they close
only when their rung's every clause is green for zoteus.

zoteus is the named target because *works for me* is the acceptance standard and
this is the tool the author runs. A green here is a green for zoteus and for
nothing else; the other four roster targets are measured by the same layer and
are not this tracker's business.

## Children

Children are filed when their work starts, not up front. The gaps below are
enumerated with the requirement sentence each rests on, which is what a child
ticket would carry anyway; filing four stubs today would inflate the queue
without advancing anything (severity floor, `rules/workflow.md`). This tracker is
edited to list each child as it is created.

